  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nOccasional rain continues through Thursday night\n\nUpper low west of MCI with southerly moist fetch east of this \nfeature will continue to lead to periodic showers across the region \nand plentiful moisture advects northward and upper disturbances move \nnortheast. Slight break will occur when the upper low churns \noverhead Wednesday morning before wrap around disturbances move \nsouthwest over the CWA as the upper low departs to the east. While \navailable moisture will be abundant today and tonight (03.12z DVN \nRAOB had a daily record 1.5\" of PWAT) forcing for ascent is in the \nform of periodic shortwaves without the presence of a stationary or \nquasi-staitonary boundary usually needed for training. Finally, \nmoisture and rain rates look to take a step downward as the upper low \ndeparts and drier air begins to filter in from the northwest. 03.12z \nREFS max rainfall reflects this with values of only around 1\" of \nrain after 18z today. Therefore, while many locations will receive \naround an additional 0.5-1\" of rain over the next couple days, \nflooding concerns are minimal.\n\nTemperatures fall and then rise\n\nProgression of the upper low over the next few days will allow for \ncooler air to push south into the region. This, combined with \noccasional light rainfall and cloudiness, will lead to temperatures \nin the upper 40s for Thursday and Friday, just a bit below normal \nfor early November. While there is a good deal of interquartile \ntemperature spread (8-10 degrees F per 03.13z NBM) Saturday and \nSunday, guidance largely points toward subtly rising heights going \ninto Monday of next week with around a coin flip (40-60%) chance for \nhighs to reach 60 degrees.",
